<TopicPage slug="subjects/architecture/advanced/structural-systems-arches-to-skyscrapers">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Structure is the part of a building you are least allowed to improvise: gravity is
    patient, wind is periodic, and the difference between a crack and a collapse is a
    load path that never loses its way to the ground. The established systems — walls,
    frames, arches, shells, cables — are a small set of <em>geometries</em> for creating
    that path, each exploiting one material strength; the history of building is their
    recombination with each material generation, from stone to steel to engineered timber.
    The skyscraper is the lineage's modern extreme and this chapter's spine: a machine for
    stacking floor area that begins as a gravity problem and becomes, past about ten
    storeys, a <strong>wind</strong> problem. The syllabus asks for two things: command of
    the systems and assemblies themselves (SC.4's established and emerging technologies),
    and the judgement to <strong>assess them against design, economics and performance
    objectives</strong> — structure as a set of priced options, not a march of progress.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the families, the frame+elevator formula, the wind inversion, the frontiers" widget="equationStep" config={structCfg} />

  <h2>The five load-carrying geometries</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Load-bearing walls</strong> carry in compression — stone's strongest mode — but
    must be thick, and thickness scales with height: Chicago's 16-storey Monadnock Block
    (1891) needed base walls 1.8 m deep, eating floor area; unreinforced masonry tops out
    near there. <strong>Frames</strong> (columns and beams) concentrate the load into
    slender verticals and free the wall to become skin — the conceptual invention that made
    the modern plan possible. <strong>Arches and vaults</strong> carry by redirecting load
    along a curved <em>thrust line</em> into pure compression: material never has to resist
    bending, so a well-shaped masonry arch can stand for millennia; the corollary is that
    the supports must resist the arch's outward <strong>thrust</strong> (flying buttresses,
    tie rods, or the buttressing mass of adjacent bays). <strong>Shells and domes</strong>
    extend the idea into doubly curved surfaces — strength from geometry, where a few
    centimetres of concrete can roof a stadium because curvature converts load into
    membrane action. <strong>Tension structures</strong> (cables, membranes, tents) invert
    the whole lineage: steel's equal strength in tension lets cables carry with essentially
    no self-weight penalty — the most material-efficient system of all, purchased with the
    least stiffness (they move, and their movement must be designed, not ignored). Every
    real building is a combination; reading a section plan is recognising which geometry
    each element is playing.
  </p>

  <h2>The frame and the elevator: how height became usable</h2>
  <p>
    Height's economics are simple — land is finite, air is free — but two inventions had to
    converge. The first is the <strong>metal frame</strong>: Chicago's Home Insurance
    Building (1885) carried its loads on iron and steel columns, so masonry became a
    weather screen rather than structure. The second is the <strong>safety elevator</strong>
    (Otis, 1852): height above the fifth floor was previously unusable, and the elevator
    converted structure into rentable floor area. Their combination produced the
    skyscraper's template, and the template's refinement is the history of the next
    century: riveted steel frames, then welded, then composite (steel beams bonded to
    concrete slabs acting together), then concrete cores coupled to perimeter frames —
    each step adding stiffness per kilogram. The low-rise building's engineer fights
    gravity; the frame's birth made height <em>possible</em>, but the next section's force
    is what makes it <em>hard</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>The wind inversion: forces that grow faster than height</h2>
  <p>
    Gravity per floor is roughly constant; wind is not. Wind pressure q = ½ρv² — at
    40 m/s (a severe storm at height), q = 0.5 × 1.25 × 1600 = 1,000 Pa, so a 50 m-wide
    tower collects a line load of 50 kN per metre of height. The <strong>overturning
    moment</strong> grows with height <em>squared</em>: for a 300 m tower under that load,
    M = w·h²/2 = 50 × 90,000/2 = 2,250,000 N·m per metre of width — resisted as a
    compression–tension couple in the plan's extreme columns 20 m apart, each carrying
    M/d ≈ 113 MN. And <strong>sway</strong> grows with height to the <em>fourth</em> for a
    uniform cantilever, which is why codes limit drift to roughly h/500 and why
    supertalls sprout stiffness rather than strength: outrigger systems engaging
    perimeter mega-columns, tube structures (Fazlur Khan's revolutionary insight — make
    the perimeter itself the lateral system, as in the John Hancock's X-braced tube),
    diagrids (the Gherkin's lattice carrying laterally by triangulation), and the Burj
    Khalifa's buttressed core (a Y-shaped plan whose wings brace the centre). Above
    ~300 m, the governing motion is often <strong>crosswind</strong>: vortices shedding
    alternately from the flanks drive side-to-side oscillation that no downwind stiffness
    fixes directly — countered instead by shaping (tapering, set-backs and porosity break
    the vortex coherence) and by <strong>tuned mass dampers</strong>: Taipei 101's
    660-tonne steel pendulum sways out of phase with the building, cancelling energy at
    the critical frequency. The lesson of the inversion: the tall building's real design
    limit is usually <em>perceived motion</em>, not stress.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> skyscrapers are made strong by thick steel. The
    supertall is a <em>stiffness</em> design: mass is the enemy (it adds seismic load and
    cost), and the game is arranging material as far from the section centre as possible —
    perimeter tubes, outriggers, buttressed cores — because stiffness grows with the
    square of the distance material sits from the neutral axis. The slenderest towers
    (432 Park Avenue's 15:1 ratio) are stiffness theatre in expensive dress: every
    element's position, not quantity, is the design.
  </p>

  <h2>Assessing technologies against objectives</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus's second demand is evaluative judgement: a technology is not "advanced"
    or "traditional" in the abstract — it is cheap or expensive, fast or slow, heavy or
    light in carbon <em>for a brief</em>. The assessment grid is explicit. <strong>Design
    objectives</strong>: spans, floor-to-floor heights, vibration limits (offices tolerate
    more motion than laboratories), future flexibility (long spans and shallow services
    buy re-planning freedom). <strong>Economics</strong>: structure is roughly 15–25% of
    construction cost; frame choice sets crane time, storey-cycle speed (concrete cores
    slipped ahead of steel erection; timber panels bolt together in days — labour and
    programme are structural properties), and floor-plate efficiency (core size as a
    percentage of plan). <strong>Performance</strong>: embodied carbon per m² (concrete
    ≈ 300–500 kgCO₂e/m² of structure; mass timber a fraction of that — the
    materials-selection lesson's indices at building scale), fire strategy (timber's
    char-rate engineering versus concrete's inherent rating), acoustics (heavy floors
    insulate), and end-of-life (demountable steel bolted connections versus glued CLT
    panels — design for disassembly is now a scored criterion). A competent brief names
    the weights; a competent engineer shows the table rather than the preference.
  </p>

  <h2>Landmark systems worth knowing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The dome's two problems:</strong> thrust outward at the base (the buttress answer) and compression rings at the crown (the oculus answer). The Pantheon (2,000 years, still the largest unreinforced concrete dome) and Brunelleschi's Florence double-shell — the catenary lesson's story continued upward.</li>
    <li><strong>Shells: strength from shape:</strong> thin concrete shells (Nervi, Candela, Utzon's opera-house sails) carry enormous loads by curvature — material efficiency that cantilevered forms cannot match; the engineering lesson's "form follows forces", at its purest.</li>
    <li><strong>Cable structures:</strong> suspension bridges (the span ladder of the bridges lesson) and stadium roofs (Wembley, Munich) — tension members pulled into pure axial efficiency; the stiffness price is movement, managed with damping.</li>
    <li><strong>The next system being tested:</strong> 3-D-printed structures, robotic assembly, and demountable design-for-disassembly (the circular-economy lesson applied to buildings) — structure meets lifecycle thinking.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The tall-building systems ladder, quantified.</strong> Fazlur Khan's famous
    chart plots the economic height ceiling of each system: rigid frames reach ~20 storeys,
    shear-truss–frame interaction ~40, framed tubes ~60, trussed tubes and bundled tubes
    ~100+ (the Willis Tower is nine bundled tubes of differing heights), outrigger systems
    and buttressed cores beyond 150. The driver is the square and fourth-power laws above:
    each system buys more <em>plan-width stiffness</em> per kilogram. High-strength
    concrete (80–100+ MPa) contributes by shrinking columns whose pure gravity load —
    hundreds of MN at the base of a supertall, piled floors upon floors — would otherwise
    consume the plan; the Burj's base columns carry more axial force than a destroyer's
    displacement, in compression, for decades, with creep (the materials ladder's
    time-dependent strain) designed for explicitly.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Emerging assemblies.</strong> Mass timber (cross-laminated timber panels,
    glulam frames) has reached 18-storey towers (Mjøstårnet, 85.4 m) with hybrid
    timber-concrete floors answering vibration and acoustic mass; its constraints are
    connection engineering, moisture management during construction and fire engineering
    by char calculation. Robotic and 3-D-printed construction promise geometric freedom
    (material placed only where forces go — the shell lesson's ethic at automation speed)
    while raising QA questions about layer anisotropy and connection robustness. Damping
    is becoming active: distributed smart dampers and control forces (the control-systems
    lesson's MPC applied to a building) blur structure into machine.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> How should codes treat performance-based seismic
    design (accepting controlled damage in rare earthquakes rather than strength for the
    worst case)? What is the honest carbon ledger when timber's sequestered carbon and
    concrete's cement emissions both depend on forestry and clinker-substitution
    assumptions decades out? And as towers climb past 1 km (Jeddah Tower's stalled
    attempt), does any physics remain or only economics — elevators (core area grows with
    height as lift traffic compounds), construction logistics, and the fundamental
    habitability of motion at the top? The structural families are stable knowledge; their
    economics are what keep being rewritten.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Salvadori, <em>Why Buildings Stand Up</em>; Ali &amp; Moon, "Structural developments in tall buildings" (2007); CTBUH tall-building database; the statics, materials-selection and structural-engineering lessons of this atlas.</p>
